{
  "image_name": "0c71ce49505e18651df41e42d7f29187.png",
  "prompt_text": "The figure summarizes a three-phase model of how regulatory T cells establish immune tolerance. In the first phase, set in a lymph node, a Treg cell interacts with an antigen-presenting cell (APC) and appears to inhibit APC function. This is shown through CTLA4 and TCR/MHC-related contacts that reduce the APC’s stimulatory capacity and limit activation of nearby effector T cells.\n\nIn the second phase, the Treg cell creates a suppressive microenvironment. It is associated with anti-inflammatory or regulatory signals such as IL-10 and other cytokines, while also consuming IL-2 via CD25. Surface enzymes CD39 and CD73 convert ATP into adenosine, adding another suppressive pathway. The figure also highlights integrin αvβ8, which activates latent TGFβ into active TGFβ. Together these mechanisms promote local suppression and support Treg expansion.\n\nThe third phase depicts longstanding tolerance. Active TGFβ and expanded Treg populations sustain suppression over time, while effector T cells are kept in check. The label “infectious tolerance” suggests that once established, this tolerant state can spread or reinforce itself by generating additional regulatory activity. Overall, the image presents tolerance as a stepwise process from APC inhibition to a stable, self-perpetuating suppressive immune state.",
